UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 40, Lesson 2: Reflection and celebration', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Celebration shares learning highlights and thanks helpers, then sets one next curiosity to investigate — joy with evidence, not only scores.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same science idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite a celebration caption for one artefact and one next question you still wonder about.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCaption names what it shows; question is investigable later.\n\nWATCH FOR\nCelebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nHost a mini showcase (table or screen). Present one artefact caption, one thank-you and one next question. Parent/teacher sign-off on the next question optional.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Science and Technology","learning_intention":"Celebration shares learning highlights and thanks helpers, then sets one next curiosity to investigate — joy with evidence, not only scores.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply and make","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe everyday objects for observation"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["ST2-SCI-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Host a mini showcase (table or screen). Present one artefact caption, one thank-you and one next question. Parent/teacher sign-off on the next question optional. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about celebrate linked to that evidence.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample misconception: Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community.. Correction: Caption names what it shows; with evidence.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses celebrate. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one science misconception about celebrate, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about celebrate. Reject title-only labels with no observation. / Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for celebrate. Reject wording changes that leave the same error..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for celebrate. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Caption names what it shows","Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community"],"question":"Write a celebration caption for one artefact and one next question you still wonder about. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for celebrate; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of share and explain why it matters with clear evidence","Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community","Celebration shares learning highlights and thanks helpers, then sets one next curiosity to investigate — joy with","Caption names what it shows"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with celebrate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named share and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community","Name share and explain why it matters only, without linking it to celebrate","Apply celebrate to new material and name the share and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies celebrate with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Trustworthy science claims rest on observable evidence linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community","Use observable features or measurements as evidence, not appearance alone","Decide from which object looks most exciting","Copy a label from the lesson title with no observation"],"question":"New examples: a charging phone, a nesting bird, a glass marble, a fallen dry leaf. Which claim about celebrate is most trustworthy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Science claims need named observations linked to the claim. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A label copied from the title with no evidence","Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community","A named observation plus how it supports the claim","A longer story with no observation"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about celebrate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about celebrate with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Caption names what it shows","Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster"],"question":"Using this science idea — Celebration shares learning highlights and thanks helpers, then sets one next curiosity to — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about celebrate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["If the observed feature changes, the claim about celebrate should change with it","Any exciting story about the object must be true","Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community","Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded"],"question":"Which prediction about celebrate can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about celebrate. Reject title-only labels with no observation.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses celebrate. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about celebrate linked to that evidence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for celebrate. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about celebrate, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community.. Correction: Caption names what it shows; with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y3-w40-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise celebrate.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations.","Say what each observation shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Working Scientifically and Technologically","success_criteria":["I can use celebrate with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify share and explain why it matters.","I can practise gratitude independently on a new prompt.","I can notice next needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Celebration shares learning highlights and thanks helpers, then sets one next curiosity to investigate — joy with evidence, not only scores.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","Celebration shares learning highlights and thanks helpers, then sets one next curiosity to investigate — joy with","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about celebrate with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nShare board: favourite graph, proud prototype photo, kind thank-you to a partner who helped debug. Next curiosity: “How do worms help soil?”\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite a celebration caption for one artefact and one next question you still wonder about.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCaption names what it shows; question is investigable later.","explain":"Celebration shares learning highlights and thanks helpers, then sets one next curiosity to investigate — joy with evidence, not only scores.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Celebration focus.","caption":"Kindness required.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Share</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">artefact highlight</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Thanks</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">partner/helper</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Share board: favourite graph, proud prototype photo, kind thank-you to a partner who helped debug. Next curiosity: “How  Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Share board: favourite graph, proud prototype photo, kind thank-you to a partner who helped debug. Next curiosity: “How  Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.","options":["A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test","Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about celebrate with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nShare board: favourite graph, proud prototype photo, kind thank-you to a partner who helped debug. Next curiosity: “How do worms help soil?” Look at each observation, variable or design choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the science idea visible: name the observation, property or test step you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show scientific thinking. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Closing showcase.","caption":"Keep joy with evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Next</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">curiosity question</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Showcase</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">caption + thanks</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation or test","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about celebrate with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCelebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community. Here is a more productive model: Share board: favourite graph, proud prototype photo, kind thank-you to a partner who helped debug. Next curiosity: “How do worms help soil?”\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nCaption names what it shows; question is investigable later.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Celebration that mocks others’ results breaks the learning community. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about celebrate with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nCelebration shares learning highlights and thanks helpers, then sets one next curiosity to investigate — joy with evidence, not only scores. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\